\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs24\b\i WINDOWS-SPECIFIC FINDING: the EInvalidOp crash is now permanently fixed upstream (superseding this file's previous revision)\i0\b0\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22
The previous revision of this report needed an uncommitted, locally-patched copy of newVMbench.lpr (a {\f1 Math.SetExceptionMask(...)}\f0  call added to its main block) to get past a crash in the single-precision complex section at N=100. That patch has since been made permanent and correct, and this run used the plain, unmodified newVMbench.lpr with no workaround of any kind.\par
The root cause turned out to be different from what the previous revision of this report assumed. That revision's finding (inherited from an even earlier investigation) blamed PurePascalLUC's Gaussian elimination, based on a comment in newVMComplex.pas/newVMComplexSingle.pas claiming LinearSolveZ/LinearSolveC "always" run the PurePascal path regardless of build (true only when HAVE_ACCELERATE is defined - Darwin-only - since that's the platform where Accelerate's zgetrs_/cgetrs_ has a genuine crash bug forcing the PurePascal path unconditionally). That comment is stale: the actual code has a proper {\f1 \{$IFDEF PUREPASCAL\}/\{$ELSE\}}\f0  split, and on this Windows machine's normal library-backed build (PUREPASCAL undefined), LinearSolveZ/LinearSolveC genuinely call {\f1 lapacke_zgetrs/zgesv}\f0  and {\f1 lapacke_cgetrs/cgesv}\f0  - real MKL calls, not PurePascalLUC at all.\par
This was confirmed empirically this session, not assumed: newVMbench.lpr was temporarily instrumented with a WriteLn+Flush(Output) after every MatMultC/LinearSolveC/InvertC call, rebuilt, and rerun. The crash landed precisely between the "before LinearSolveC" and "after LinearSolveC" markers at N=100 - i.e. inside {\f1 lapacke_cgesv}\f0  itself (MKL's own compiled code), not in any Pascal code this library controls. The instrumentation was reverted immediately after (git diff clean) once the finding was captured.\par
FPC's Windows runtime leaves the FPU's invalid-operation/denormal/underflow traps unmasked by default - unlike Linux/macOS, where the C runtime MKL links against already masks them as a hardware/OS-level default. MKL's LAPACKE complex solve/factor routines occasionally produce a transient denormal or borderline value internally during pivoting - numerically harmless, silently flushed/rounded on Linux/macOS - but that trips a hard, process-aborting EInvalidOp on Windows. The fix (oneapi.pas, commit 6f2ed6f) masks the same set of FPU exceptions the earlier workaround used ({\f1 exInvalidOp, exOverflow, exZeroDivide, exUnderflow, exPrecision, exDenormalized}\f0 ), once, in OneAPI.pas's existing Windows-only {\f1 initialization}\f0  section, immediately after the MKL/IPP function pointers are resolved - the same place LoadMKLFunctions/LoadIPPFunctions already run. This is a genuine upstream fix (not a benchmark-local patch): it applies to every program that links this library on Windows, not just newVMbench, and brings Windows' FPU behaviour in line with what Linux/macOS already provide by default rather than weakening anything those platforms had.\par
A useful side-effect of tracking this down properly: the earlier revision of this report also flagged an "unexplained" timing discrepancy for LinearSolveZ/LinearSolveC at N=1000 (14ms vs 853ms; 8ms vs 3304ms) between the two builds, despite believing both ran identical PurePascal code. That mystery is now resolved, not just re-explained - it was never an anomaly. LinearSolveZ/LinearSolveC genuinely run a fast library-backed LAPACKE path in the "library" build and the slower PurePascalLUZ/LUC path in the "PUREPASCAL" build, exactly like every other operation in this report; the previous report's confusion came from trusting the stale banner text over the actual compiled code. See the per-type tables below, where LinearSolveZ/LinearSolveC now show ordinary, unremarkable speedup numbers in line with LinearSolve/Invert's own trend.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\b Notes:\b0\par
Residuals are Frobenius-norm ||A*x-b|| (solve) / ||A*Ainv-I|| (invert) / round-trip ||IFFT(FFT(A))-A|| (FFT) / ||A*v-lambda*v|| worst-case over all eigenpairs (EigDecompose); both builds compute the same correct answer to within precision-appropriate tolerance in every case. Speedup = PUREPASCAL time / library-backed time (a value below 1.00x means the library-backed call was actually SLOWER at that N - this happens for several small/medium-N cells below). All timings in milliseconds, single run per row (not averaged) - see the EigDecompose section for a visible example of the run-to-run noise this implies at sub-millisecond scale.\par
N=10 anomalies: several "first LAPACKE/FFTW call of the process" cells are wildly out of line with their neighbours - LinearSolve(real double) N=10 costs 5.31ms library-backed vs a sub-resolution 0.00ms PUREPASCAL (lapacke_dgesv is the first LAPACKE_* call the whole process makes); FFT(double) N=10 costs 0.68ms library-backed vs 0.01ms PUREPASCAL for the same reason (FFTW's fftw_plan_dft_1d pays a one-time plan-creation cost on its first call). These n/a-marked cells are excluded from the speedup column as meaningless rather than reported as a literal (near-infinite) multiplier.\par
\fs22\par

