\pard\page

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs26\b Summary\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22
As expected, the gap between library-backed and PUREPASCAL grows sharply with N for every O(N^3) operation. MatMult sees the largest speedups among the linear-algebra trio (137x-539x at N=1000, MKL/OpenBLAS's cblas_?gemm vs a plain triple loop, single-precision complex reaching furthest since it also carries the biggest PurePascal constant-factor penalty per element). LinearSolve is again the least favourable to the library at small/medium N for the two real types - N=100 actually runs FASTER in PUREPASCAL there (0.16x and 0.70x "speedup") - before the library pulls ahead again at N=1000 (35x-64x). The two complex types no longer show that particular dip (LinearSolveZ/C's N=100 speedups are a modest but real 1.73x/6.80x), which fits: their library branch calls the combined-factor-and-solve lapacke_?gesv only once per fresh matrix, same as the real types, but complex arithmetic's larger per-element cost means the PUREPASCAL triple loop's own overhead is comparatively more expensive at N=100, tilting the crossover point earlier than the real types show. Invert lands in between the two, at 60x-539x depending on type.\par
FFT/IFFT show by far the largest speedups of anything in this report (up to 685x for single-precision FFT at N=1000) - this comparison compounds two effects at once: FFTW's O(N log N) algorithm against PPDirectDFT's/PPDirectDFTS's O(N^2) direct summation, on top of the usual hand-tuned-library-vs-plain-Pascal constant-factor gap the other operations show. EigDecompose shows the most modest and least consistent gains of any operation tested (0.85x-2.10x across N=50/100, plus two small-N cells below 1.00x that are more plausibly single-run noise than a real trend - see that section's own note) - LAPACKE's dgeev/sgeev carry comparatively high fixed overhead relative to the ported EISPACK-style Balance/ElmHes/Eltran/Hqr2/BalBak pipeline at these matrix sizes; the library's advantage would be expected to grow at larger N (untested here - see the N=1000 exclusion rationale above), consistent with every other operation's trend in this report.\par
Residuals confirm both variants compute the same correct answer at each N, precision, and operation (double ~1e-15 to 1e-11 for the LU-based trio, ~1e-15 to 1e-12 for EigDecompose, ~1e-15 to 1e-11 for FFT round-trip; single correspondingly looser at ~1e-6 to ~5e-2), consistent with the other two platforms' logs for the operations they share.\par
Unlike the previous revision of this report, there are no outstanding open items here: the Windows-only EInvalidOp crash is fixed upstream (oneapi.pas, commit 6f2ed6f) rather than worked around locally, and the LinearSolveZ/LinearSolveC timing discrepancy flagged previously as unexplained is now understood to have been the genuine, expected library-vs-PUREPASCAL speedup all along - see the WINDOWS-SPECIFIC FINDING section above for both.\par
